<body>
<h1>Unit 15: Refraction at Plane Surfaces</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Waves &amp; Optics</p><p class="summary">Refraction at a plane boundary is Snell's law, but the interesting physics is in the consequences: the lateral shift through a slab, the shallower apparent depth of a pool, and total internal reflection, which is the whole basis of fibre optics.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Snell's law reads n1 sin(theta1) = n2 sin(theta2), with n = c/v the refractive index; the frequency of light is unchanged at the boundary while its speed and wavelength change.</li><li>A plane-parallel slab displaces the emergent ray sideways while keeping it parallel to the incident ray; the displacement grows with thickness and with obliquity.</li><li>Total internal reflection requires n1 &gt; n2 and an incidence angle above the critical angle theta_c = arcsin(n2/n1), about 41.1 degrees for crown glass (n = 1.52) against air.</li><li>Viewed near-normally, an object in water appears shallower by a factor of about n, so a 1.5 m depth reads as about 1.1 m.</li><li>Optical fibres work by repeated total internal reflection; the acceptance angle is fixed by the numerical aperture of the core and cladding.</li><li>In the wave picture refraction follows from phase continuity at the boundary: the phase must match across it, which is Snell's law in a different costume.</li><li>Snell's law is a statement about phase continuity across a boundary: the change of direction is what keeps the wavefronts in step, and the same law governs light as a wave and as a quantum field.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Snell's law assumes an isotropic, homogeneous, stationary, non-absorbing medium and a smooth interface; rough or moving boundaries change the result.</li><li>Near the critical angle the transmitted ray grazes the surface and the reflection coefficient approaches one.</li><li>The simple apparent-depth rule (divide by n) holds only for near-normal viewing; at oblique angles the correct factor is cos(theta')/cos(theta).</li><li>Dispersion - the wavelength dependence of n - is neglected in the basic model but must be included whenever white light is involved.</li><li>Fermat's principle fixes the path of least time rather than least distance, and that distinction is why a refracted ray bends towards the normal in a denser medium.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Refractive index of crown glass</td><td>about 1.52</td></tr><tr><td>Refractive index of water</td><td>about 1.333</td></tr><tr><td>Critical angle, crown glass to air</td><td>about 41.1 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>Critical angle for water to air</td><td>about 48.6 degrees</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can an image be formed that is not limited by the diffraction limit?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Superlenses using negative-index materials beat the diffraction limit at a single frequency; extending the idea to a usable bandwidth remains an open and difficult problem.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does light slow down in a medium without losing energy?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The classical account through the material's dielectric response is standard, while the fully microscopic picture of coherent forward scattering is subtler.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the lateral shift of a ray through a glass slab and verify the prediction with a pencil, a glass block and a sheet of paper.</li><li>Design an optical fibre for a given numerical aperture and explain the role of the cladding.</li><li>Explain why a swimming pool looks shallower at the far end, and derive the apparent depth for oblique viewing.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-3-refraction">OpenStax: Refraction</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-4-total-internal-reflection">OpenStax: Total Internal Reflection</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
